{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310521,"nonce":"tu7MGV8mHf75Yy2C7HY7hu/FCdY=","title":"An abstract whose underlying type never resolves is given a 32-bit INTEGER slot, and abstract-method writes to `this` are silent no-ops; ...","message":"Three stacked defects, verified twice independently (mechanism reproduced down to\narm64 machine code). NOT the Single(f32) family — the wrong slot is an **integer\ni32**, not an f32. See [[bugs_single_plus_f64_literal_broken_three_of_four]] for\nthat separate one.\n\n1. `compiler/src/ir/mir/types.rs:245` — an abstract whose `underlying` is `None`\n   falls back to `IrType::I32`, while EVERY sibling unresolved arm in the same\n   match (Dynamic, Unknown/Error, GenericInstance, Placeholder, None) returns\n   `Ptr(Void)` with a comment saying it does so \"to avoid truncating\". Two\n   independent things leave `underlying` None: the abstract being **generic**\n   (`Val\u003cT\u003e(T)` never substitutes the type argument), and the abstract being\n   **declared after** the class that uses it. `private` and the `:Float`\n   constraint are irrelevant.\n   DECISIVE PROBE: `abstract Val(String)` declared after the class prints\n   `1023423968` — a pointer truncated to 32 bits. `typedef Val = Float` and a\n   plain `static var v:Float` are both clean.\n\n2. `compiler/src/ir/mir/decl/signatures.rs:183-191` — an abstract method's `this`\n   is parameter 0 with `by_ref: false`, no write-back, and `inline` is not\n   honoured at the call site. So inside an abstract method `this++`,\n   `this = x` and `this = this + 1.0` are ALL silent no-ops, with no diagnostic,\n   even when the storage width is correct. `b += 1.0` on the variable works.\n\n3. The mac PASS is a REGISTER COINCIDENCE, not correctness. `unit.Test.eq\u003cT\u003e`\n   erases to `(*void, i64, i64)`; `cranelift_backend.rs:3384-3409` has coercion\n   arms for I32-\u003eI64, I8-\u003eI64, int-\u003eF64, int-\u003eF32 but **none for F64-\u003eI64**, so an\n   f64 is pushed into an i64 ABI slot unchanged (and `enable_verifier` is false in\n   release, so nothing catches the CLIF type violation). On arm64 the scratch\n   `mov x0,x19` leaves x0 holding the same operand that lands in x1, so the callee\n   compares 10 against 10 and reports equal. x86-64 uses different scratch\n   registers and does not coincide.\n\n**Issue4649's honest verdict is FAIL on every platform.** Do not treat the mac\nPASS as a baseline to protect.\n\nNOT TRUE, checked directly and it does not reproduce: \"float comparison is\ndegenerate\". Direct `==` and an erased-generic `eq\u003cT\u003e` on plain Floats both give\ncorrect answers on both tiers. The degeneracy is confined to the broken-abstract\noperand.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_abstract_with_unresolved_underlying_gets_i32_slot` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}